-- GAME ----------------------------------------------------------------------
  Title       Olli & Lissa: The Ghost of Shilmoore Castle
  Publisher   Firebird, 1986
  Version     1.0

-- YOU NEED ------------------------------------------------------------------
  Your own copy of the original game, saved beside this patch
  (one of these files):

  File        Olli & Lissa - The Ghost of Shilmoore Castle (1986)(Firebird
              Software).tzx
  MD5         741249bc06ef95e2359e9bf878117394

  File        Olli & Lissa - The Ghost of Shilmoore Castle (1986)(Firebird
              Software)[a].tzx
  MD5         0bdd2f6ca2237e727e93a90903f9b25b

  The patch carries none of the game's code. It only works with that exact
  dump, under that name or any other: anything else is refused, with a
  message naming the file it wants.

  Memory      Any Next: a 1MB Next has the memory for the game. Enhanced needs
              a 1MB Next, and is not offered on a smaller one.

-- WHAT IT DOES --------------------------------------------------------------
  Moving figures become Next hardware sprites, so they keep their own colours
  and no longer take on the scenery's (colour clash). Game logic is unchanged.

  Colours     Default (the game's own) plus: Enhanced, chosen on the menu, off
              by default.
  Enhanced    All 68 moving figures painted: Olli green, Lissa pink, Sir
              Humphrey and the ghosts white, the creatures in their own
              colours. A first pass. Off by default. A painted set, colourist
              not named.

  Pace        original

  Note        Every moving figure (Olli, Lissa, Sir Humphrey, the castle's
              creatures) is a hardware sprite laid on its own pixels, so it no
              longer takes the colours of the room it crosses; the game
              underneath is unchanged. Default: the figures in white with a
              black edge. Enhanced paints them. The pick-ups and the title's
              pictures do not move and stay as the game draws them. Keys:
              Enter starts; the game's own redefine screen sets the keys.

